<handout>
<title>Rational Equations</title>
<page>
    <objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solve rational equations.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Check solutions.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </objectives>
    <p>
        We will use a similar technique to solve a rational equations as we did in the last section to simplify. When we multiply by the <term>least common denominator (LCD)</term>,
        it will clear all of the fractions leaving us with an equation without fractions. For this class, that will mean we are left with a linear or quadratic equation. 
    </p>
    <p>
        There will be one additional step after solving a rational equation. In the process of solving, we could have canceled out a factor that ends up leading to an 
        extra solution. We need to check all of our solutions in the original equation. Solutions that make the original equation true are true
        solutions. Solutions that make the original equation undefined are called <term>extraneous solutions</term>.
    </p>
    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation: <m>\frac{1}{x+2}+1 = \frac{5}{x+2}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>x=2</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Start by multiplying each term by the LCD <m>x+2</m>
                <md>
                    \frac{1(x+2)}{x+2}+1(x+2) = \frac{5(x+2)}{x+2}
                </md>
                Cancel out common factors, leading to a linear equation
                <md>
                    1 + x+2 = 5 \rightarrow x = 5-2-1 = 2
                </md>
                We need to check the solution in the original equation to make sure it is a true solution.
                <md>
                    \frac{1}{2+2}+1 = \frac{5}{2+2} \rightarrow \frac{1}{4} + 1 = \frac{5}{4} \rightarrow \frac{5}{4} = \frac{5}{4}
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
</page>
<page>
    <example workspace="3.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation: <m>\frac{8}{x-2} = -\frac{10}{2x+7}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>x=-\frac{18}{13}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Start by multiplying each term by the LCD <m>(x-2)(2x+7)</m>
                <md>
                    \frac{8(x-2)(2x+7)}{x-2} = -\frac{10(x-2)(2x+7)}{2x+7}
                </md>
                Cancel out common factors, leading to a linear equation
                <md>
                    8(2x+7) = -10(x-2) \rightarrow 16x+56 = -10x+20 \rightarrow 26x = -36 \rightarrow x = -\frac{36}{26} = - \frac{-18}{-13}
                </md>
                We need to check the solution in the original equation to make sure it is a true solution. Using a calculator:
                <md>
                    \frac{8}{(-18/13)-2} = -\frac{10}{2(-18/13)+7} \rightarrow -\frac{26}{11} = -\frac{26}{11}
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
